<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stolac</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stolac is a small town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, on the Bregava river. Its surroundings are rich in archaeological remains, including the Illyrian hillfort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daorson">Daorson</a> and the medieval necropolis of Radimlja, whose decorated tombstones (stećci) are part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage listing. The town's Ottoman mosques and bridges were badly damaged in the war of the 1990s and have since been restored.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stolac.jpg" alt="Stolac" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Golden Bosnian Lily · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stolac_(collage).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Stolac is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, in the Herzegovina-Neretva Canton of the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. It stands on the tourist route that links the mountainous hinterland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> with the coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, along the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a>, Stolac, Ljubinje and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebinje">Trebinje</a>. Its natural setting, climate and hunting grounds attracted settlers from prehistory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> all left remains. It is described as one of the oldest continuously inhabited towns in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been settled for at least 15,000 years, according to markings in Badanj Cave that experts have dated to between 12,000 and 16,000 BCE. The mainstream view holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daorson">Daorson</a> is 2,500 years old, though some geological studies place the ruins beneath the site at 7,000 years. Three kilometres west of the town lies the Radimlja necropolis of stećak tombstones, dating from the 15th and 16th centuries. They are carved with epitaphs, portraits of the dead, and motifs such as grapevines, hunting scenes and wild animals, and five of them are thought to mark the graves of members of the Hrabren Miloradović Vlach family.</p><p>The historic core of Stolac has nine historical layers, from prehistory through the Illyrian-Roman period, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, the Ottoman and Austro-Hungarian periods and the first and second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. The town belonged to four empires, the Roman, Byzantine, Ottoman and Austro-Hungarian, and to three kingdoms, and its people held to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. During the Yugoslav Wars a number of monuments were demolished by Croat extremists as part of a campaign of ethnic cleansing, including the town's four mosques, which dated from the 16th to 18th centuries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> of the Holy Assumption of Christ.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 2003 the Commission to Preserve National Monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> designated Stolac Old Town a National Monument, allowing only research, conservation and restoration. A dispute followed over a series of crosses, the Way of the Cross, built within the old town from the war years onward, which the Commission and the Federation Ministry for Spatial Planning objected to. After the Dayton peace treaty the territory of the 1991 municipality was divided between Stolac and the new municipality of Berkovići in Republika Srpska, where most of the Bosnian Serbs from Stolac now live. A road of 36 kilometres linking Stolac with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neum">Neum</a> was completed in 2022.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stolac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 3rd century BCE they had a fortified centre at Daorson, on a hill near modern <b>Stolac</b>, enclosed by massive Cyclopean walls.</blockquote><a href="/state/daorsi">Daorsi</a></li></ul></section>
